WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.297
Subqueries become useful when one part of the question depends on the result of another query.

00:00:05.297 --> 00:00:10.062
Sometimes the inner query produces one benchmark such as average age.

00:00:10.062 --> 00:00:13.357
Sometimes it produces a set of patient IDs.

00:00:13.357 --> 00:00:18.097
Sometimes we only need to know whether a matching related row exists.

00:00:18.097 --> 00:00:24.392
And sometimes the inner query depends on the current outer row.

00:00:24.392 --> 00:00:29.047
These are different contracts, and recognizing the contract is more useful than memorizing syntax.

00:00:29.047 --> 00:00:34.822
Connect this idea to an e-commerce team finding customers who purchased a target product.

00:00:34.822 --> 00:00:40.582
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:40.582 --> 00:00:48.167
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:48.167 --> 00:00:53.712
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:53.712 --> 00:00:59.280
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
